rubric.description:
Top scores should show:

- logical valuation sequencing;
- targeted diligence prioritization;
- appropriate use of AI for synthesis and scenario testing; and
- explicit awareness of deal dependencies.

Top scores should show:

- correct DCF construction;
- correct treatment of D&A, CapEx, NWC, taxes, and UFCF;
- correct discounting and Terminal Value mechanics;
- correct Enterprise Value interpretation;
- awareness of the Equity Value bridge; and
- ability to explain the model verbally.

rubric.anchorStrong:
Top scores should show:

- correct DCF construction;
- correct treatment of D&A, CapEx, NWC, taxes, and UFCF;
- correct discounting and Terminal Value mechanics;
- correct Enterprise Value interpretation;
- awareness of the Equity Value bridge; and
- ability to explain the model verbally.

Track anchor:
Links the strategic rationale to valuation, constructs the DCF correctly, triangulates rather than blindly selecting one valuation method, identifies the assumptions driving value, explains the model clearly, and materially re-underwrites the deal after the legal shock. Strong candidates recognize that legal uncertainty can affect both expected cash flows and the appropriate level of confidence in the valuation.

rubric.description:
Top scores should show:

- skepticism toward revenue-only valuation claims;
- independent checking of AI-generated calculations or assumptions;
- explicit identification of unsupported legal conclusions; and
- willingness to reject an attractive answer when the evidence does not support it.

Strong candidates use AI to:

- compare valuation cases;
- summarize diligence materials;
- identify potential sensitivities;
- test alternative scenarios; and
- pressure-test a recommendation,

without outsourcing the actual investment judgment.

